he data set  ran it and then submitted my uh result  so um that's yeah so  in a short time frame i think um if  you're training models every day  like this is something that can  immediately help you  and then if you want to learn more i  finally  got around to publishing a paper about  h2o automl uh just last month  it's the tool has been out there for  about three years  um you know i spent a lot of time on  documentation and things like that and  not so much time on a paper but i did  finally  write one so if you want to read more  about the algorithm and see how it  scales  i did a study from like data sets of 10  000 rows all the way up to 100 million  rows  and that's not a single machine by the  way so we are  very able to scale um well basically  that's that's why  uh you know building an auto ml tool on  a library like h2o  is was appealing to me because the base  models themselves or the underlying  models are quite fast  so i only have one minute so i'm just  going to leave you with a few links  the last one is a talk that i just gave  at the usar  last month and this is all about  responsibility  uh of of data scientists so the title  was like responsible  automation and like how do basically how  do we be  responsible humans when we're building  these autumn automl tools and just  machine learning tools in general so  there's a lot of discussion about  interpretability and algorithmic  fairness and stuff like that so i think  i'm out of time so i will just  leave you with some contact info and a  shout out to our leads  we're just going to stop there cool  thanks jared  thank you for speaking and yes notice uh  the  the strong our lady's presence here  there's always been a good showing thank  you all for coming thank you everyone  for coming
 

